Got it, so touching endpoints don't merge, only a real overlap does. So I sort by start, then merge only when the next start is strictly less than the current end. Let me code that up.

```python
def merge_intervals(intervals):
    if not intervals:
        return []
    intervals.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])
    merged = [list(intervals[0])]
    for start, end in intervals[1:]:
        if start < merged[-1][1]:
            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], end)
        else:
            merged.append([start, end])
    return merged
```

Sorting is O(n log n), the single pass is O(n), so overall O(n log n) time and O(n) space for the output. The one change from the sheet is that strict `<` instead of `<=`, so [1, 4] and [4, 6] stay separate.

